WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.869 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  they traveled from across the country to  the capital khartoum where the Accord  was signed many gathered at the newly  renamed Freedom Square once the site of  protest rallies against the former  President Omar al-bashir as Al Jazeera's  Heba Morgan reports from Khartoum the  deal paves the way for a civilian-led  government after 18 months of protests  and four months of negotiations Sudan's  ruling military junta and the opposition  coalition known as the forces of freedom  and change inked a final power-sharing  deal marking the start over thirty nine  month transitional period I stand before  you with pride after we have overcome  the stage of preparation and progressed  to the stage of construction we chant  together with honest hearts that the  focus now is on the nation a nation that  is proud of you and you Evette a country  that has produced people who have raised  its name with the generosity and  achievements the final agreement  includes a transitional period led for  the first 21 months by the military and  for the remaining 18 months by civilian  from the opposition coalition it also  includes members for an Executive  Council nominated by the FFC and they  will also have control of two-thirds of  the seats in the Legislative Assembly  but the coalition that has negotiated  the deal has other challenges some of  its own members have refused to  recognize the agreement saying it  doesn't address the core issues of  conflict in the country other members  say bringing peace will be one of the  top tasks of the transitional government  when the issue of war and peace is one  of the most important priorities of the  coalition and we worked to tie the issue  of peace to the issue of democratic  transformation because we believe in the  right to live in peace we believe in the  rights of the martyrs the displaced  people and refugees we affirmed that the  FFC aims for a comprehensive peace in  all areas of conflict  outside the hall people celebrated the  hope for a new phase in Sudan's history  but with cautious optimism the signing  of the agreement on Saturday may mark a  landmark on the road to a civilian rule  but many Sudanese say it's not the end  of the revolution they say the struggle  will have succeeded only when the  military hands over power and justice  and accountability begins more than 250  deaths have been recorded since the  start of the protests in December with  at least 120 killed in June when the  military raided the pro-democracy sit-in  at Army Headquarters which had become  the symbol of the revolution the next  step we are waiting for is the justice  for the blood of martyrs before power is  handed to the civilians and before the  economy improves justice for those who  sacrificed their lives so that the whole  country can be happy like they haven't  been for the past 30 years we don't  expect the road ahead to be easy but  hope to reach the end together the  things people lost their lives for the  demands must be met it's a first step  and that's what we're celebrating not  the end of our revolution on the day of  the signing hundreds arrived in the  capital from AD para where the protests  began in December before spreading  across the country the chants reflected  their optimism about Sudan's future but  also included a reminder to the new  transitional government that they're  ready to keep the flames of their  revolution burning till all their  demands are met Heba Morgan al Jazeera  Khartoum

